localserver_generic_task_prompt.other:
Use the TeamBuddy Skill [$teambuddy]({{.SkillPath}}) for this local task. Read the Skill file first, or load it through the native Skill mechanism if supported.

Task UUID: {{.TaskUUID}}
Tool type: {{.Tool}}
Execution index (execution_index): {{.ExecutionIndex}}
回传通道：script

{{.OutputDirSection}}

Copy the task UUID above verbatim; command arguments must match character by character. Do not retype it from visual memory or substitute similar characters. If the script reports a UUID argument validation failure, compare the actual command with the original prompt first. Only when a transcription error is confirmed, correct it using the original UUID and retry once; never guess a new UUID. Argument validation fails before any HTTP request and does not indicate network permission issues, a missing task, a binding conflict or an expired execution index.

This tool uses a generic execution binding and does not require a native session ID. Read the task and all history, verify the execution index, then follow the Skill’s generic tool binding procedure to obtain a report ID issued by the client. Never invent thread_id or read another conversation’s binding files.

After binding, report user messages, final answers and task status on subsequent turns. Use work directories returned by the task API. If local file access, script execution or access to the local client is unavailable, explain that binding and reporting are unavailable; do not claim success.

Before connecting, check this tool’s documented local execution capabilities and network permissions. If access is restricted and the current policy permits a native permission request, use its actual parameters to request only the minimum access to the runtime address; retry the original request once after approval. If no permitted request is available, approval is denied, or the retry fails, pause binding and reporting and state the failed stage, address and actual error. Do not bypass the restriction with curl, another execution entry point or sandbox configuration changes. Switching execution methods or refreshing the execution index cannot resolve network permission restrictions; do not recommend either as a connection fix.

localserver_codex_task_prompt.other:
Use the current tool's native Skill loading mechanism with [$teambuddy]({{.SkillPath}}) to handle the TeamBuddy local task.

Task UUID: {{.TaskUUID}}

Tool: {{.Tool}}

Execution generation (execution_index): {{.ExecutionIndex}}

{{.OutputDirSection}}

First verify that the task's execution generation matches the value above, then read the task and its history, then follow the Skill instructions to obtain and bind the current real session ID. If no ID can be obtained, do not bind, omit thread_id, and still report user messages, the final answer, and status; do not invent an ID. The binding request type must equal the tool. For Codex, thread_id is read only from the CODEX_THREAD_ID environment variable (on Windows, $env:CODEX_THREAD_ID); this is the thread ID provided by the current session, do not guess or scan old sessions. Claude Code, CodeBuddy and Qoder use the current session ID; Pi reads PI_SESSION_ID from the current native shell. If no real session ID can be obtained, do not bind, omit thread_id, and still report user messages, the final answer, and status; continue handling the business request.

This session stays bound to the task; subsequent turns sync user messages, final reply, and task status according to the Skill rules until the conversation ends.

localserver_codex_task_prompt_manual.other:
First read the local Skill file [$teambuddy]({{.SkillPath}}) in full, then follow it to handle this TeamBuddy local task.

任务 UUID：{{.TaskUUID}}

工具类型：{{.Tool}}

执行代次（execution_index）：{{.ExecutionIndex}}

回传通道：mcp

会话 ID：{{.ThreadID}}

{{.OutputDirSection}}

要求 / Requirements:
1. Call teambuddy_get_task to read the task and its full history, and confirm the returned execution_index matches the value above.
2. Call teambuddy_bind_session to bind this session: type must be {{.Tool}} and thread_id must be the session ID given above. That is the only session ID allowed for this run — do not read environment variables, scan past sessions, or invent another UUID.
3. Call teambuddy_update_status with in_progress when the real work starts, and done only after it is finished and verified.
4. For every new user message, first report the original text with teambuddy_report_activity using type=question; before sending each final reply, report the full text using type=result. Use a distinct event_id per activity.
5. If the MCP tools are missing, the call is rejected, or the result is teambuddy_unavailable, forbidden, task_not_found, conflict, or bind_rejected, stop binding and reporting immediately and explain why. Do not work around it through another channel, and do not claim the activity was reported.

This session stays bound to the task; keep syncing user messages, final replies and task status every round.

localserver_cli_skill_prompt.other:
[$teambuddy-im-bot-cli]({{.SkillPath}})

Use the teambuddy-im-bot-cli Skill to handle this TeamBuddy {{.ChannelType}} channel fallback task: when a {{.ChannelType}} message is not recognized as a known command, TeamBuddy creates a dedicated task and forwards it to the local CLI.

Bound task UUID: {{.TaskUUID}}
Task title: {{.TaskTitle}}
Task status: {{.TaskStatus}}
User message: {{.Message}}

Follow the flow described by the teambuddy-im-bot-cli Skill. When finished, post the result back to the {{.ChannelType}} channel via /api/local/cli-skill/chat-reply (the request body MUST include channel_type="{{.ChannelType}}" so the result is routed to the correct channel). Use /api/local/cli-skill/messages or /api/local/cli-skill/activities to report intermediate progress or failures to TeamBuddy while processing.

This session is bound to the {{.ChannelType}} task for as long as it runs. TeamBuddy will notify the user automatically if the CLI exits or is cancelled.

